description:
**Problem:** After an error, page.reload() is not aggressive enough to reset ChatGPT's state. Cascading failures continue.

**Current code in src/server.js processRequest():**
```javascript
} catch (e) {
  console.log('[ask-question-server] Error occurred, reloading page to reset state...');
  await page.reload({ waitUntil: 'domcontentloaded' });
  throw e;
}
```

**Recommended fix:** Close and recreate the page instead of reloading.

```javascript
async function hardRecover(err) {
  try { if (page && !page.isClosed()) await page.close(); } catch {}
  page = null;
  // Page will be recreated on next request via ensurePage()
}
```

**Why:** ChatGPT's SPA can have stuck state (websockets, React state, focus traps) that survives a reload. Closing the page guarantees a fresh start.

**If failures persist:** Also recreate the browser context:
```javascript
try { await context.close(); } catch {}
context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: STORAGE_STATE_FILE });
```

Reference: /tmp/stackexchange-codereview-playwright-request-queue-answer.md

**Problem:** When CLI disconnects (retry or user cancel), server continues processing the request anyway. Wastes resources and causes duplicate work.

**Solution:** Hook request abort/close events and pass abort signal to work.

**Implementation:**
```javascript
async function handleRequest(req, res) {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  // Cancel work if client disconnects
  req.on('aborted', () => controller.abort());
  res.on('close', () => controller.abort());

  try {
    const response = await queueRequest(data.prompt, {
      ...opts,
      signal: controller.signal,
    });
    // ...
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.name === 'AbortError') {
      console.log('Client disconnected, cancelled work');
      return;
    }
    // ...
  }
}
```

**Files:** src/server.js handleRequest(), possibly queueRequest() and processRequest()

**Note:** Can't truly cancel mid-flight Playwright operations, but can skip before starting or bail early in queue.

